<StandardPageLayout title="About Painting for Beginners" description="Painting for Beginners helps ordinary people choose the right materials, prepare the surface, and complete practical home, furniture, and canvas projects.">
  <h2>For the person holding the brush</h2>
  <p>Painting advice often assumes you already know which product to buy, what “cutting in” means, or why a surface needs primer. We start one step earlier. Each guide explains the decision, the reason behind it, and the point where a product label or qualified professional should take over.</p>
  <p>The goal is not to make every project look effortless. It is to make the work understandable enough that you can plan it, recognize a problem, and keep going without feeling talked down to.</p>
  <h2>What we cover</h2>
  <ul><li>Rooms, walls, trim, doors, cabinets, and other household surfaces.</li><li>Furniture and useful objects made from wood, laminate, metal, and related materials.</li><li>Paint, primer, brushes, rollers, preparation, cleanup, and common mistakes.</li><li>Beginner acrylic and canvas painting for people making art for enjoyment.</li></ul>
  <h2>How recommendations work</h2>
  <p>Information comes before products. A useful supply recommendation should say what the tool does, when it is optional, and what characteristics matter. Painting for Beginners does not currently publish paid product links. If that changes, compensated links and sponsored relationships will be disclosed clearly near the recommendation.</p>
  <h2>Safety and scope</h2>
  <p>Our guides provide general educational information. Buildings, coatings, and regulations vary. Always follow current manufacturer labels and local requirements. Stop when a project may involve lead, asbestos, structural damage, active moisture, unsafe access, or another condition requiring testing or qualified help.</p>
</StandardPageLayout>
